# {{.Name}}

Production deployment guide for the `{{.Module}}` service generated by
gin-bear. The default scaffold stays minimal; everything below only exists
because this project was created with `bear new --profile production`.

## Configuration priority

Identical to the framework runtime order:

1. `application.yaml` (base, committed)
2. `application-<env>.yaml` selected by `BEAR_ENV` (`GIN_MODE=release` implies
   `prod`), plus the documented compatibility filename rule
3. `config.json` (optional local override, never committed with secrets)
4. Environment overrides (`BEAR_SERVER_PORT`, `BEAR_AUTH_JWT_SECRET`,
   `POSTGRES_*`, `REDIS_*`, ...)

Inspect resolution without starting anything: `bear doctor` (static) or
`bear doctor --probe` (with bounded dependency dials).

## Secret injection

No production secret lives in this repository. Provide them per environment:

- `BEAR_AUTH_JWT_SECRET`: 32+ random bytes when `auth.enabled=true`.
- `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` (or the full DSN your deploy platform injects).
- `REDIS_PASSWORD` when Redis requires auth.

The example files use `${VAR}` placeholders on purpose; replace them in your
deploy manifests, never by committing real values here.

## Database migration is a separate deploy step

The server never migrates on startup. Apply reviewed SQL explicitly:

```sh
make migrate            # runs ./cmd/migrate against application.yaml
```

`migrations/` files are append-only: never edit or rewrite a version that has
already been applied anywhere; add a new version instead. Rolling the
application binary back does not roll the database back; database rollback is
a separate, explicitly approved operation.

## Health checks and Graceful shutdown

- Liveness: `GET /live`. Readiness: `GET /ready` (fails while dependencies
  are unreachable).
- The server handles `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`: it stops accepting, drains
  in-flight requests within `server.shutdown_timeout`, then exits. The
  container entrypoint execs the server so signals reach PID 1:

```sh
docker run --read-only --tmpfs /tmp my-registry/{{.Name}}:latest
```

## Logs and tracing

Structured logs go to stdout (collect them with your platform). Tracing is
opt-in via the `tracing` config block (`stdout` for local, OTLP for
collectors); spans and logs redact secrets, tokens, and DSNs by default.

## Backup and restore responsibility

The framework owns no backups. The database operator owns them:

- Nightly logical backup plus WAL/PITR per your RPO.
- Restore drills against a disposable database before you need them.
- Keep one known-good migration version recorded per deploy.

## Upgrades

1. Regenerate or rebase onto the new framework version in a scratch copy.
2. Run `bear gen api ... --dry-run` equivalents and review the diff.
3. `go test ./...`, `make docker`, boot against a disposable database,
   apply migrations, smoke CRUD, then `TERM` and confirm a clean exit.
4. Promote the image tag; keep the previous tag for instant rollback.

Base images are pinned in `Dockerfile` (`golang:1.26.6-bookworm`,
`gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot`). Bump them together with the
`go` directive, rebuild from scratch, and re-run this checklist.
